WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.140
Ideally parents let their children&nbsp;
learn how to help themselves.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.260
Parents who neglect their children or those&nbsp;
who are overly involved, achieve the opposite.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.660
Their children often learn helplessness&nbsp;
— and hence believe that they can neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.600
control their life, nor change their environment.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.620
Learned helplessness was first discovered by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.760
two researchers who conditioned&nbsp;
dogs to endure electric shocks.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.600
Martin Seligman and Steven F. Maier conducted&nbsp;
their experiment with three groups of dogs.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:43.080
Dogs from the first group were caged and&nbsp;
made to wait for a period of time before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.160
being released. These dogs were&nbsp;
what's known as the control group.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:55.320
The second group were also caged and got electric&nbsp;
shocks from below. When they pressed a button,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.980
the shocks stopped. They learned&nbsp;
that they could stop the pain.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:06.120
The third group of dogs were exposed to the&nbsp;
shocks, but had no way of stopping them.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.980
After the conditioning, all three groups were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.640
put into a box that gave shocks&nbsp;
on one side, but not the other.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.400
The first and second group of&nbsp;
dogs quickly learned to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.900
being shocked by jumping over the wall&nbsp;
that separated the two sides of the box.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:32.160
The third group of dogs made no attempt&nbsp;
to avoid the shocks -they had developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.660
a cognitive expectation that they&nbsp;
could not avoid the pain. Seligman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.660
and Maier attributed this to learned helplessness.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:45.120
Children can also be affected, as&nbsp;
this oversimplified story attests.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.100
This is the story of Joe. While&nbsp;
other kids were allowed to run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.060
this cute little boy was put in a&nbsp;
walker so he couldn't hurt himself.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.640
And so, from early on, Joe learned&nbsp;
that the world is a dangerous place.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:06.900
On the rare occasions that his father was home, he&nbsp;
told Joe to toughen up, and become a strong boy.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.700
But without a role model, Joe&nbsp;
lacked the motivation to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:16.020
up to his dad’s expectations.&nbsp;
He began to feel inferior.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.500
Over time this feeling got stronger.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.940
Whenever there was a problem his&nbsp;
mother would jump in to help.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.540
Joe began to internalize the belief that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.240
couldn’t help himself when&nbsp;
confronted with challenges.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.640
Whenever he had to make a major&nbsp;
decision, he’d ask his mom for advice.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.820
Deep inside he resented her for not&nbsp;
allowing him to become more independent.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.880
What are your thoughts? How can we help&nbsp;
those that fail to see the path out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:53.460
their toxic life? How can we help them&nbsp;
to help themselves? Share your thoughts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.600
and your own experience with learned&nbsp;
helplessness in the comments below!

